{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/chess at b86eeb8b6ac7df32ec174496924e325ddc7c2278 in the gitseq-chess repository, one commit atop item-4 head 28e1146b. The implementation report for request 747adca9 under promise bbb9a922.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES. Custody was check-then-use throughout: ensureKey read a key after asking whether the name was a symlink, and created one after checking ancestors by name, so anything able to move a path component between question and answer redirected where the key was read or written. The path now runs through an os.Root held open on the bounding directory, so every name resolves against a descriptor rather than a string. os.Root refuses a link that leaves the root and follows one that does not, so each in-root refusal is explicit: name the thing, open it, prove with os.SameFile that both answers describe the same file. Static link refused outright; a link swapped mid-operation can pass only by resolving to the very inode that was named. Same shape for the chess directory. An existing key readable by anyone but its owner is refused. A new key is written beside its destination and hard-linked into place, so the real name never holds a half-written key and a concurrent publisher cannot be overwritten. The published mode is set on the descriptor, because the mode passed to open is umask-filtered.\n\n--key keeps its escape hatch and states its boundary: the operator chooses the location and everything above the file's own parent; the program pins that parent and still refuses a final symlink, a non-regular file, a loose mode, an inode that changes underneath it, and any non-exclusive publication.\n\nInvitation secrets leave argv. --join-secret and --secret are gone; --join-secret-file and --secret-file read a bounded secret from a file or from standard input when named \"-\". Naming a source that yields nothing is an error, not an absent secret.\n\nCONDITIONS MET, against the request's list: default creation stays beneath the Git common directory; symlink traversal rejected for both the key and its directory; acceptable permissions required on an existing key; atomic publication at mode 0600; --key preserved and documented with the checks that still apply; secrets accepted through stdin or a named file with the local threat model written down; mutation-sensitive tests for symlinks, permissions, interrupted and concurrent publication, and secret input; README updated.\n\nWHAT I WILL NOT CLAIM. Two race guards have no deterministic witness and I am not calling them covered: the os.SameFile identity check, and tolerating a concurrent creation of the chess directory. Both fire only when something moves mid-operation. I chose not to add a nondeterministic test that would pass by luck, and asked codex whether it wants seams for them; that question is open.\n\nHOW THE TESTS WERE CHECKED. Not by being green. Reverting any of these fails a test by name: both symlink refusals, the permission check, publication by link rather than overwrite, the explicit mode under a umask that can actually narrow it, the empty-secret refusal, and stdin reaching create and join. Two of my own tests were found worthless during this work and fixed: a umask of 0177 cleared bits 0600 does not have, so that test could never fail; and an interruption test that wrote an unrelated temporary file did not touch publishKey's real boundary, so it now injects at a link seam.\n\nCODEX PRE-AUDITED THIS HEAD BEFORE I ASKED and found eight defects across several rounds, all adopted. That is why this artifact exists at all in a state worth reviewing.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt clean, go vet clean, GOOS=windows go vet clean, go test ./... clean, go test -race ./... clean, git diff --check clean, tree clean, branch request/chess-key-custody pushed to origin.\n\nScope stayed inside cmd/chess and README.md. No lower-layer defect required a child request.","body":{"commit":"b86eeb8b6ac7df32ec174496924e325ddc7c2278","path":"cmd/chess"}}